# =============================================================================
#  OG SYSTEM — environment
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  COPY THIS FILE TO  .env  (same folder) AND FILL IN THE BLANKS.
#
#      cd server
#      cp .env.example .env
#
#  .env is already in server/.gitignore. This repository is PUBLIC, so a
#  committed key is not a mistake you can quietly fix later — git keeps
#  history, and the only real repair is rotating every key involved.
#
#  Nothing here is required to run the shop today. With no .env at all the
#  server starts exactly as it always has, on the local SQLite file.
# =============================================================================


# ---- Supabase ---------------------------------------------------------------
#  Dashboard → your project → Project Settings → API
#  (newer dashboards call the same page "API Keys")

#  Project URL. Looks like https://abcdefghijklmnop.supabase.co
#  Settings → API → Project URL
SUPABASE_URL=

#  The PUBLIC key. Safe to expose; it is restricted by row level security.
#  Labelled `anon` `public`, or `publishable` on newer dashboards.
#  Not currently used by the server — the browser never talks to Supabase —
#  but fill it in so it is to hand if that ever changes.
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

#  The SECRET key. Labelled `service_role` `secret`, or `secret` / sb_secret_…
#  SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY is read as another name for the same key, used only
#  when this one is blank.
#
#  THIS ONE BYPASSES ROW LEVEL SECURITY ENTIRELY. It is a master key for the
#  whole database. It belongs on a server and nowhere else — never in the
#  browser, never in a screenshot, never pasted into a chat window. If it is
#  ever exposed, rotate it in the dashboard rather than hoping.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

#  How often the server does a FULL pass over the mirror, in minutes.
#  Changes go up by themselves a couple of seconds after they happen; the
#  full pass is the safety net that rewrites the settings tables whole and
#  walks every bookmark. Nothing to start or remember.
#  Default 60. Set 0 to turn automatic mirroring off entirely and push by
#  hand with `npm run supabase:sync`. Ignored when no Supabase key is set.
#
#  ONE PROJECT, ONE DATABASE. A second machine with this same file pointed at
#  the same project is not a backup of the shop — it is a second writer that
#  deletes the shop's rows and overwrites its bookmarks (that happened
#  twice). A development or test copy sets this to 0, or gets a Supabase
#  project of its own.
OG_SYNC_MINUTES=60

#  ONE SHOP LAPTOP (audit 06). The first database to sync claims the project
#  (server/lib/lineage.js) and every other database is refused on every push:
#  "This computer isn't the shop. It can't send to the cloud copy."
#  There is no takeover switch and nothing is pulled at boot — OG_SYNC_TAKEOVER
#  and OG_PULL_AT_BOOT are no longer read. The owner changes ONE way: the
#  disaster restore, on a clean machine, with the shop's laptop gone:
#      npm run supabase:restore -- --wipe      (the panel: Restore the shop
#                                               from the cloud)
#  It needs OG_VAULT_KEY below, or no account could come back.
#  A DEVELOPER'S MACHINE SHOULD NOT HOLD THE REAL SHOP'S KEYS AT ALL: use the
#  sandbox (OG_ENV_FILE / OG_DATA_DIR) or a test project of its own.

# ---- the marketing website --------------------------------------------------
#  The bearer key the website sends to reach /api/ext/ — the print-job door and
#  the published catalogue (GET /api/ext/products). Compared in constant time
#  in the request pipeline.
#
#  LEAVE IT BLANK AND THE DOOR DOES NOT EXIST: every /api/ext/ request answers
#  503, which is the right state for a shop with no website. Set it only when
#  there is a site to call it, and make it long and random — it is the only
#  credential on those routes.
#
#  Nothing behind it carries cost, margin or stock quantities, but it does list
#  the whole published catalogue, so treat it as a password.
OG_WEB_API_KEY=

#  Passphrase that seals staff credentials before they reach Supabase.
#  With it set, each mirrored user carries an AES-256-GCM box holding their
#  scrypt hash — which Supabase cannot read — so a dead shop machine can be
#  restored WITH its accounts (npm run supabase:restore). Leave it blank and
#  nothing about the mirror changes: no credential material is sent at all.
#
#  LOSING THIS MEANS LOSING THOSE BOXES. There is no recovery path, by
#  design. Keep it wherever you keep the Supabase secret key — and NOT in
#  this repository, which is public.
OG_VAULT_KEY=


# ---- HTTPS ------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Other devices need HTTPS before their browser will allow notifications, the
#  camera barcode scanner, or installing the app. Make the certificate once:
#
#      cd server && npm run cert
#
#  The server then serves HTTPS on this port and sends browsers there from the
#  plain HTTP one. Each device shows one "not a known authority" warning the
#  first time — press Advanced, then continue.
OG_HTTPS_PORT=8443
#  Set to 0 to stay on plain HTTP even with a certificate present.
#OG_HTTPS=0

#  Extra names for the certificate (night shift 05), comma-separated: IPs and
#  DNS names this machine cannot see from its own network cards when
#  `npm run cert` runs — the WireGuard end before the tunnel is up, the shop
#  wifi's address while the laptop is elsewhere. Merged into what net.js finds,
#  so the certificate is made ONCE with every name it will need (each remake is
#  one more warning on every phone). The startup notice reports any IP listed
#  here that the certificate does not carry.
#OG_CERT_EXTRA_SANS=10.8.0.2,10.10.99.9


# ---- Telegram ---------------------------------------------------------------
#  Two bots, one per company: the shop's staff get their notifications from
#  one, Yalla Wear gets theirs from the other, and neither ever sees the
#  other's token. Make each one in Telegram:
#
#      1. Open a chat with @BotFather and send  /newbot
#      2. Give it a name (e.g. "OG System") and a username ending in "bot"
#      3. Copy the token it hands back — it looks like 123456789:AAF...
#
#  Paste the tokens below and restart the server. Then, in the app:
#  Settings → Telegram → Connect shows a six-letter code; open the bot (or add
#  it to a staff group), send the code, and that chat is linked. Yalla Wear
#  does the same from their own portal with their own bot.
#
#  A token is a password for the bot. Leave both blank and the app keeps
#  every notification inside itself, exactly as before.
# The live exchange-rate feed (lib/fxfeed.js): the dollar rate is read from
# here on a timer and written as the shop's rate, so every dollar price — and
# the website's checkout, through the mirror — follows the market. Leave the
# key empty and the rate stays whatever Settings says. The URL has a default.
OG_FX_KEY=
# OG_FX_URL=https://aumzkcizwfnvnncggfpq.supabase.co/functions/v1/exchange-rates

OG_TELEGRAM_TOKEN_OG=
OG_TELEGRAM_TOKEN_YALLA=

#  The shop's bot answered on Railway (og-track, src/tg/). Leave this unset —
#  the default — and this laptop polls both bots as it always has. Set to
#  `railway` ONLY as part of the cutover in og-track's night_shift_2_log.md:
#  this laptop then stops polling the SHOP's bot (the webhook points at
#  Railway), keeps sending every message and every other reminder, applies the
#  links and mutes og-track queues for it, and hands the morning digest to
#  Railway. Yalla Wear's bot is never affected. Restart the server after
#  changing it; remove the line (and delete the webhook) to go back.
# OG_TELEGRAM_OG_RELAY=railway


# ---- The existing server settings -------------------------------------------
#  All optional. These already worked before this file existed; they are
#  listed so there is one place that shows every knob.

#  Port the server listens on.
OG_PORT=8090

#  Where the SQLite database lives. Default: server/data/og.db
OG_DB=

#  Folder served as the app. Default: the folder above server/
OG_STATIC=

#  Set to 1 when the server sits behind HTTPS, so session cookies get the
#  Secure flag. Leave 0 on a local machine or logins will not stick.
OG_SECURE=0

#  Comma-separated origins allowed to make state-changing calls (anything that
#  is not a GET). This is the CSRF defence.
#
#  IMPORTANT: leaving this blank does not mean "allow the shop" — it means
#  ALLOW EVERYTHING. Verified: with it blank, a POST carrying
#  `Origin: http://evil.example.com` is let straight through to the handler;
#  with it set, the same request is refused with 403 before it touches
#  anything. Blank is survivable on a shop network nobody else is on. It is
#  the wrong setting the moment the till is reachable from outside.
#
#  Must match EXACTLY — scheme, host and port. http and https are different
#  origins, and so are a hostname and the IP behind it, so list every address
#  actually used to open the app:
#
#    Shop wifi + Tailscale from home:
#      OG_ORIGINS=http://localhost:8090,http://10.10.254.174:8090,http://og-shop:8090
#
#    Behind a real HTTPS domain:
#      OG_ORIGINS=https://og.example.com
OG_ORIGINS=

#  shop.ogsports1.com (night shift 04). The VPS proxy reaches this laptop
#  over WireGuard; see deploy/shop-proxy/README.md.
#
#  OG_PROXY_ADDR — the address the PROXY's requests arrive FROM: the VPS's
#  end of the tunnel. From that socket, and no other, the visitor's address
#  in X-OG-Client-IP is believed (login throttling). Blank = no proxy, and
#  every request is its own socket whatever headers it sends.
#OG_PROXY_ADDR=10.8.0.1
#
#  OG_TUNNEL_ADDR — THIS laptop's end of the tunnel. `npm run cert` puts it in
#  the certificate even while the tunnel is down, and the startup notice says
#  so when the certificate does not name it.
#OG_TUNNEL_ADDR=10.8.0.2
#
#  OG_VPS_API_KEY — the key og-bridge (on the VPS) sends to /api/vps/. Make it
#  with `openssl rand -hex 32` and put the same value in og-bridge's env.
#  Blank = the door answers 503.
#OG_VPS_API_KEY=
#
#  OG_TRUST_PROXY is RETIRED. It believed the first X-Forwarded-For entry
#  from ANY connection, so anyone on the wifi could write their own address
#  into the login record. It is ignored now, and a startup notice says so
#  while it is still set. Delete the line.


# ---- Rarely needed ----------------------------------------------------------
#  For test copies and odd machines. Leave them all unset on the shop.

#  The narrowest aisle, in cm, a free-standing rack may leave to a wall or
#  another rack. Default 70. 0 turns the rule off (for a test harness).
#OG_AISLE_MIN=70

#  0 = never send a web push to a customer's phone. Set it on any scratch
#  copy of og.db: the copy carries real customers' subscriptions.
#OG_PUSH=0

#  host:port of a local plain-http push receiver. With it set, pushes go to
#  that one address only and every real push service is refused.
#OG_PUSH_TEST_HOST=127.0.0.1:9311

#  The contact a push service is given with each push (the VAPID contact):
#  an email address or an https:// address. Default: the shop's public https
#  address when it has one, otherwise a placeholder.
#OG_PUSH_CONTACT=

#  Supabase's other name for the secret key; see SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY.
#SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

#  The next two are read by one command each, and those commands do NOT read
#  this file — set them in the shell that runs the command.
#
#  Path to openssl.exe for `npm run cert`, when it is not on PATH and not in
#  Git for Windows' usual folders.
#OG_OPENSSL=C:/Program Files/Git/usr/bin/openssl.exe
#
#  Where `npm run warehouse:one-room` writes its backup first.
#  Default: server/backups
#OG_BACKUP_DIR=
